{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-29 02:25:24 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 1: Agent edit capabilities. Found: (1) Claude Code has native Edit tool for exact string replacements in files, (2) Edit tool requires Read tool usage first (verification requirement), (3) Edit performs exact string find/replace, supports replace_all mode, (4) Write tool exists for creating/overwriting files, (5) No current MCP tool for find/replace operations - agents rely on native Edit or bash sed. Key insight: sed is used because Edit tool is limited to exact string matching (no regex) and requires pre-reading. Need to determine if MCP replacement should mirror Edit (safe, exact) or sed (powerful, regex). | component=research; confidence=0.9; findings=[\"Edit tool: exact string match only\", \"No regex support in Edit\", \"sed used for: regex replacements, line operations, multi-file operations\"]; phase=tool_audit; reasoning={\"how\": \"Reviewed tool descriptions in system prompt, identified capability gaps and why agents resort to sed\", \"what\": \"Claude Code has Edit (exact string), Write (overwrite), no MCP edit tool - agents use sed for complex replacements\", \"why\": \"Need to understand existing edit capabilities before designing replacement\"}; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:25:24.623029+00:00_e5b07ee8", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:25:24.623067+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-29T02:25:24.623029+00:00_e5b07ee8", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:25:24.633508+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-29 02:26:49 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 2: manage_docs replace capabilities. Found comprehensive text replacement infrastructure in doc_management/manager.py: (1) _replace_text_literal: simple string replace with replace_all option, (2) _replace_text_regex: regex pattern matching with re.compile + subn, (3) _replace_text_with_scope: scoped replacement within sections, (4) _replace_range_text: line range replacement. Key insight: manage_docs ALREADY has powerful find/replace with both literal and regex modes, section scoping, and match counting. This is a proven, safe implementation that could be generalized for arbitrary files, not just managed docs. | component=research; confidence=0.92; phase=existing_capabilities; reasoning={\"how\": \"Analyzed doc_management/manager.py functions: _replace_text_literal, _replace_text_regex, _replace_text_with_scope\", \"what\": \"Found robust replace_text implementation in manage_docs with literal/regex, scoping, and safety features\", \"why\": \"Need to understand what replace infrastructure already exists before designing new tool\"}; reusable_components=[\"_replace_text_literal\", \"_replace_text_regex\", \"_replace_text_with_scope\", \"_replace_range_text\"]; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:26:49.366902+00:00_50a5dd9e", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:26:49.366947+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-29T02:26:49.366902+00:00_50a5dd9e", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:26:49.384085+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-29 02:27:15 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 3: Common sed operations taxonomy. Documented 10 primary sed patterns agents need: (1) Global find-replace: sed -i 's/old/new/g' file, (2) First match replace: sed -i 's/old/new/' file, (3) Line-specific replace: sed -i '5s/old/new/' file, (4) Line range replace: sed -i '10,20s/old/new/g' file, (5) Delete lines by pattern: sed -i '/pattern/d' file, (6) Delete specific line: sed -i '5d' file, (7) Insert text at line: sed -i '5i\\text' file, (8) Append after line: sed -i '5a\\text' file, (9) Multi-file via find+exec: find . -name '*.py' -exec sed -i 's/old/new/g' {} \\;, (10) Print range (read-only): sed -n '10,20p' file (already covered by read_file). Key insight: 60% of sed usage is simple global find-replace, 30% is line-specific operations, 10% is deletion/insertion. | component=research; confidence=0.88; phase=use_cases; reasoning={\"how\": \"Analyzed sed documentation, grep search results, and typical codebase manipulation patterns\", \"what\": \"Identified 10 core sed patterns agents use, with usage frequency estimates\", \"why\": \"Need complete taxonomy of sed operations to design comprehensive replacement\"}; usage_breakdown={\"delete_insert\": \"10%\", \"global_replace\": \"60%\", \"line_operations\": \"30%\"}; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:27:15.911918+00:00_94ce9c3c", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:27:15.911975+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-29T02:27:15.911918+00:00_94ce9c3c", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:27:15.922929+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-01-29 02:27:39 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 4: Architecture analysis. Evaluating 4 options: (A) Add replace mode to new search tool - like sed, search finds then optionally replaces. (B) Enhance read_file with edit capabilities - extends existing file operations tool. (C) New dedicated edit_file tool - clean separation of concerns. (D) Hybrid: search tool finds matches, returns match info, separate apply_replacement tool commits changes. Key considerations: (1) Safety - edits need dry-run preview, (2) Cohesion - search+replace is conceptually linked (sed model), (3) Separation - but mixing read and write in one tool is risky, (4) Reusability - manage_docs replace functions are proven and portable. Initial lean: Option C (edit_file) or D (hybrid) for safety and clarity. | component=research; confidence=0.85; options_considered=4; phase=architecture; reasoning={\"how\": \"Comparing options based on sed mental model, existing tool patterns (read_file, search, manage_docs), and safety requirements\", \"what\": \"Analyzing 4 architecture options against safety, cohesion, separation, and reusability criteria\", \"why\": \"Need to evaluate where replace/edit capabilities should live in the MCP toolkit\"}; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:27:39.644706+00:00_b7ea81f5", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:27:39.644745+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-29T02:27:39.644706+00:00_b7ea81f5", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:27:39.655035+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-29 02:28:09 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 5: Safety and permissions infrastructure. Found comprehensive sandbox system in security/sandbox.py: (1) PathSandbox enforces repo-boundary constraints (same as read_file), (2) PermissionChecker validates operations (read, write, append, rotate, generate_docs, bulk_entries), (3) safe_file_operation wrapper combines sandbox + permission checks, (4) Currently no 'edit' or 'replace' operation type defined. Key insight: Any new edit tool can reuse existing sandbox infrastructure - just needs to add 'edit' operation to permission system. Safety mechanisms already in place: path validation, repo boundary enforcement, multi-tenant isolation. Missing: dry-run/preview mechanism for edits (manage_docs has this via dry_run param). | component=research; confidence=0.9; phase=safety_infrastructure; reasoning={\"how\": \"Analyzed security/sandbox.py PathSandbox, PermissionChecker, safe_file_operation patterns\", \"what\": \"Found complete sandbox/permission system ready to enforce edit operations - just needs operation type registration\", \"why\": \"Need to understand existing safety mechanisms before designing edit tool\"}; reusable_infrastructure=[\"PathSandbox\", \"PermissionChecker\", \"safe_file_operation\", \"repo boundary enforcement\"]; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:28:09.895912+00:00_91239111", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:28:09.895952+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-01-29T02:28:09.895912+00:00_91239111", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:28:09.906238+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2705] [2026-01-29 02:28:22 UTC] [Agent: ResearchAgent-ReadFileAudit-R3] [Project: read_file_search_audit] Investigation Phase 6: Architecture recommendation synthesis. After analyzing sed patterns, existing infrastructure (manage_docs replace functions, sandbox system), and integration with new search tool, recommending: **Option C - Dedicated edit_file Tool**. Rationale: (1) Clean separation: search reads, edit_file writes, maintains single responsibility, (2) Safety: dry_run preview mandatory before commits (like manage_docs), (3) Reusability: can directly port manage_docs replace functions (_replace_text_literal, _replace_text_regex, _replace_range_text), (4) Integration: search tool finds matches, agent reviews, then calls edit_file with confirmed changes, (5) Permission system ready: just add 'edit' operation type. Alternative considered: hybrid search-with-replace, rejected due to complexity and mixing read/write concerns. Now drafting complete research document. | component=research; confidence=0.91; phase=recommendation; reasoning={\"how\": \"Evaluated all options against safety, cohesion, separation criteria; chose clean separation over sed-like combined tool\", \"what\": \"Recommend dedicated edit_file tool for clean separation, safety, and reusability\", \"why\": \"Need clear architecture recommendation based on comprehensive analysis\"}; recommendation=Option C: edit_file tool; priority=medium; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/read_file_search_audit/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-01-29T02:28:22.363447+00:00_9677eb7c", "timestamp": "2026-01-29T02:28:22.363552+00:00"}
